@h1 Nodes & core vocabulary h1@

One mechanic underlies every structure in Wit: a @strong node strong@ wraps
content between an opening and a matching closing name.

@@@pre
@h1 A title h1@

@blockquote A quotation, set apart. blockquote@
pre@@@

The opening is @@@code @name code@@@, the close is @@@code name@ code@@@, and the
body is everything between. Nodes nest inside each other, and indentation is only for
your eye — scope comes from the open/close pairs, never from whitespace.

@h2 The built-in vocabulary h2@

You don't define these — they ship: headings, lists, quotes, tables, figures,
links, sectioning, and more. See the
@a |href ../../reference/core-vocabulary/| core vocabulary reference a@ for the
full set, each with the element it produces.

@h2 Parameters carry metadata h2@

Anything that describes a node — a link's address, an image's size — rides in
@strong parameters strong@, kept separate from the body content.

@@@pre
@a |href https://example.org| the link text a@
pre@@@

Here @em the link text em@ is the content; @code href code@ is metadata. That
one rule — content in the body, metadata in parameters — is what keeps Wit
documents clean as they grow.
